  I'm trying to achieve the following:

  three panels with a categorical x and numeric y, data in each panel
have very different scales so I want to use free scales. I then want to
flip coordinates to get categorical variables on the y axis.

  I'm using coord_flip() rather than putting the numeric variable on x
and the categorical variable on y in the first place because I want to
use geom_pointrange().  I'm using facet_grid() rather than facet_wrap()
because I want to suppress the y-axes on the all but the leftmost planel.

I'm guessing that what I'm trying to do doesn't work because before
coord-flipping the y (numerical) scales of the grid must be lined up.

The only solutions I can think of are:
  (1) write a geom_hpointrange() that takes xmin and xmax rather than
ymin and ymax and generates a horizontal segment through the points;
  (2) use geom_point() and geom_segment() separately to get the same effect.

  I'd like to put in a request, by the way, for a geom_linespoints()
that would combine geom_line() and geom_point() -- it would be a little
easier than e.g. repeating
stat_summary(...,geom="point")+stat_summary(...,geom="line") [if there
can be a geom_pointrange() that combines geom_point() and
geom_linerange(), then we might as well have geom_linespoints() too ...]

  Is there any change to the status of baptiste's comment at

http://stackoverflow.com/questions/7029906/extending-ggplot2-properly

  that the best way to figure out how to write a new geom is to look at
the source code?  In other words, has anyone put together a "Writing
Geoms for Dummies" document ... ?

 thanks
   Ben Bolker

